# Who is asked to look at a change to a document that records a decision.
#
# What this file does here today: GitHub requests a review from the owner automatically when a
# pull request touches one of these paths. That is routing, and it is worth having - the ladder
# opens pull requests unattended, and an unrequested review is one nobody is waiting for.
#
# What it does NOT do here today: block anything. Both rulesets carry
# `"require_code_owner_review": false` and `"required_approving_review_count": 0`
# (`gh api repos/design4pro/money-tracker.online/rulesets/22037820`, and `22037770` for `main`),
# and neither branch has classic protection - the REST endpoint answers `404 Branch not
# protected`. A CODEOWNERS entry with that setting off is a suggestion.
#
# Turning `require_code_owner_review` on is a separate decision with a trap worth naming: GitHub
# does not let anyone approve their own pull request, so with a single code owner every governance
# change that owner writes would need the `OrganizationAdmin` bypass - which teaches the habit of
# bypassing. A change to these paths is `risk-high` by convention and a person merges it
# (`SDLC.md`, ADR 0026).

